#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <limits.h>

void findMax() {
    int size;
    scanf("%d", &size);

    if (size <= 0 || size > 10000) {
        printf("Invalid input size. It should be within range [1, 10000].\n");
        return;
    }

    int *array = malloc(sizeof(*array) * size);
    if (array == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to allocate memory.\n");
        return;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        long value; // use long to detect integer overflow during input
        scanf("%ld", &value);

        if (value < INT_MIN || value > INT_MAX) {
            printf("Integer overflow detected for value: %ld\n", value);
            free(array);
            return;
        }

        array[i] = value;
    }

    int max = INT_MIN;
    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        if (max < array[i]) {
            max = array[i];
        }
    }

    printf("Largest integer is: %d\n", max);
    free(array);
}
```
In this code, we first read the size of the list from standard input. If it's not within valid range [1, 10000], an error message will be printed and the function terminates early. Then, we allocate a dynamic array to store integers according to given size. If allocation fails, another error message is printed and function returns immediately. We then read each integer from standard input, checking for overflow before storing it in the array. Once all values are read and stored, we find the maximum value in the array by iterating over it and updating `max` if necessary. Finally, we print the largest integer found and free the allocated memory.
