['Here's an example of how you might implement this in C. This code is a simplified version and does not include all features you requested such as error handling or sophisticated synchronization techniques. However, it should serve as a good starting point for your task. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdarg.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

enum LogLevel { LOG_INFO, LOG_WARNING, LOG_ERROR };

static enum LogLevel currentLogLevel = LOG_INFO; // default log level
static pthread_mutex_t lock;  // global mutex for thread safety

void setLogLevel(enum LogLevel newLevel) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    currentLogLevel = newLevel;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

void logMessage(enum LogLevel level, char *fmt, ...) {
    if (level < currentLogLevel) return;

    va_list args;
    va_start(args, fmt);

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);  // lock to ensure thread safety

    if (level == LOG_INFO) fprintf(stderr, "[INFO] ");
    else if (level == LOG_WARNING) fprintf(stderr, "[WARN] ");
    else if (level == LOG_ERROR) fprintf(stderr, "[ERROR] ");

    vfprintf(stderr, fmt, args);  // print to console

    FILE *file = fopen("log.txt", "a");
    if (!file) {
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
        return;
    }

    if (level == LOG_INFO) fprintf(file, "[INFO] ");
    else if (level == LOG_WARNING) fprintf(file, "[WARN] ");
    else if (level == LOG_ERROR) fprintf(file, "[ERROR] ");

    vfprintf(file, fmt, args);  // print to file

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);  // unlock after writing to console and file
    fclose(file);
}
```
In this example, we use a global mutex for ensuring thread safety. `setLogLevel` allows you to change the logging level at runtime, while `logMessage` logs messages with the appropriate log level prefix based on the current log level setting. It also prints both to console and file simultaneously. Note that fprintf is used instead of snprintf in this example due to simplicity, but you can easily replace it with a proper implementation if required.

This code does not handle large numbers of logging entries efficiently as it does not use any buffering or other techniques for performance optimization. For a production-ready solution you would need to implement these features yourself, possibly using a thread-safe queue data structure for buffering the log messages, and also taking advantage of operating system's own buffered I/O mechanisms (such as sendfile) if they are available.
