["```cpp
#include <forward_list>
using namespace std;
void replaceOrAppend(forward_list<int>& flist, int target, int replacement) {
    auto prev = flist.before_begin();
    for (auto it = flist.begin(); it != flist.end(); ++it) {
        if (*it == target) {
            *it = replacement;
            return;
        } else {
            prev = it;
        }
    }
    flist.insert_after(prev, replacement);
}
```
This function uses a simple loop to search for the first occurrence of the target integer in the forward list. It replaces the target with the replacement if found, otherwise appends the replacement to the end of the list. 

It's worth noting that we are assuming here that insertion is a thread-safe operation, and so this function should be safe for use in a multi-threaded environment as long as no other operations on the forward_list are done concurrently. However, if you need to support such scenarios, you might want to look into using synchronization primitives or locking mechanisms provided by your platform (like mutexes, locks etc.).
