["Here's a simple implementation using Node.js, Express and MongoDB:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
const bodyParser = require('body-parser');

// Connect to your database
mongoose.connect('mongodb://localhost/permissionSystem', {useNewUrlParser: true, useUnifiedTopology: true});

const roleSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
    name: String,
    permissions: [String]
});

// Create a model based on the schema
const Role = mongoose.model('Role', roleSchema);

const app = express();
app.use(bodyParser.json());

// Handle PUT request to update existing role's permission
app.put('/roles/:id', async (req, res) => {
    const updatedRole = await Role.findOneAndUpdate({_id: req.params.id}, {$set: {permissions: req.body.permissions}}, {new: true});
    if (!updatedRole) return res.status(404).send('The role with the given ID was not found');

    res.send(updatedRole);
});

// Handle DELETE request to delete a role
app.delete('/roles/:id', async (req, res) => {
    const deletedRole = await Role.findByIdAndRemove(req.params.id);
    if (!deletedRole) return res.status(404).send('The role with the given ID was not found');

    res.send(deletedRole);
});

// Handle POST request to create a new role
app.post('/roles', async (req, res) => {
    let newRole = new Role({
        name: req.body.name,
        permissions: req.body.permissions
    });

    newRole = await newRole.save();

    res.send(newRole);
});

// Handle GET request to fetch roles with pagination and filtering
app.get('/roles', async (req, res) => {
  const pageSize = 10;
  const pageNumber = parseInt(req.query.pageNumber || 1);

  // Filtering criteria can be passed as query parameters
  let filterCriteria = {};
  if (req.query.name) filterCriteria['name'] = req.query.name;
  if (req.query.permissions) filterCriteria['permissions'] = {$in: [req.query.permissions]};

  const roles =  await Role.find(filterCriteria).skip((pageNumber - 1) * pageSize).limit(pageSize);

  res.send(roles);
});

const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(port, () => console.log(`Listening on port ${port}...`));
```
This example is quite simple and assumes you have a MongoDB database running locally with a 'roles' collection. Also note that this does not handle potential concurrency issues (like race conditions) in the update or delete operations. A more robust solution would likely involve using transactions, optimistic concurrency control strategies, or some other form of locking to prevent simultaneous updates/deletes on the same role.
"]

['Sure, here is a basic implementation in C# using System.Xml.XPath namespace to parse an XML document with XPath. The LINQ-to-XML or XmlDocument might be other suitable options depending on the requirements. Please note that this is a simplified example and doesn't take all your needs into account:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Xml.XPath;

public class XmlParser
{
    private readonly XPathDocument _document;
    private readonly Dictionary<string, XPathNavigator> _cache = new Dictionary<string, XPathNavigator>();

    public XmlParser(string xml)
    {
        try
        {
            var xpathDoc = new XPathDocument(xml);
            _document = xpathDoc;
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Error parsing XML: " + e.Message);
        }
    }

    public List<XPathNavigator> GetElementsByName(string name, string secondaryAttribute)
    {
        if (_cache.ContainsKey(name))
        {
            return new List<XPathNavigator>{_cache[name]};
        }

        var nav = _document.CreateNavigator();
        var iterator = nav.Select("/descendant::" + name);
        var elements = new List<XPathNavigator>();

        while (iterator.MoveNext())
        {
            elements.Add(nav.Clone());
        }

        if (!elements.Any()) throw new Exception("No element found with the given name");

        // If secondary attribute is provided, sort by it. Otherwise return all matching elements in document order.
        if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(secondaryAttribute))
        {
            var ordered = elements.OrderBy(e => int.Parse(e.GetAttribute(secondaryAttribute, string.Empty)));
            _cache[name] = ordered.First();
            return ordered.ToList();
        }

        _cache[name] = elements.First();
        return elements;
    }
}
```
This is just a simplistic demonstration and might not cover all your needs for sure, but it's a good starting point to build on and make adjustments according to your needs. This code doesn't handle namespaces, you would need to modify the XPath expressions accordingly. Also, it assumes priority attribute contains integer values, if it can be other types, you would need to adjust parsing of that attribute as well. 

Remember to add proper error handling and logging for production-ready applications.
']

['Here is how you can implement it in Java. It uses Apache Commons library for computing MD5 hashes. 

```java
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import org.apache.commons.codec.binary.Hex;
import org.apache.commons.codec.digest.DigestUtils;

public class MD5Hasher {
    private AtomicLong hashComputations = new AtomicLong(0);

    public synchronized Map<String, String> getMD5HashMap(String... strings) {
        try {
            Map<String, String> result = new HashMap<>();
            for (String str : strings) {
                result.put(str, DigestUtils.md5Hex(str));
            }
            hashComputations.addAndGet(strings.length);
            return result;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("An error occurred while computing MD5 hashes.", e);
        }
    }

    public long getTotalHashComputations() {
        return hashComputations.get();
    }
}
```
This class uses `AtomicLong` to track the total number of hash computations performed, and `synchronized` to ensure thread safety. It creates a map for each input string, calculates its MD5 hash using `DigestUtils.md5Hex()` from Apache Commons library, adds it to the map with the corresponding string as key, increments the total number of computations by the length of the strings array (assuming one computation per string), and finally returns the map.

Remember that MD5 is a cryptographic hash function with a collision resistant property and it's not suitable for hashing passwords or other sensitive data where you need a secure but non-reversible transformation. You should consider using stronger hashing algorithms like SHA-256 if possible.
']

