  }

  readConfigFileSync(filePath, encoding=this.defaultEncoding) {
    if (!fs.existsSync(filePath)) {
      console.error('The file does not exist:', filePath);
      return {};
    }

    const resolvedPath = path.resolve(__dirname, filePath); // Resolve relative paths to absolute ones
    encoding = encoding || this.defaultEncoding;

    if (this.cache[resolvedPath]) {
      return this.cache[resolvedPath]; // Return from cache if it exists
    }

    let fileContent;
    try {
      fileContent = fs.readFileSync(resolvedPath, encoding); 
    } catch (err) {
      console.error('Error reading the file:', err);
      return {};
    }

    if (!fileContent || !fileContent.trim()) {
      this.cache[resolvedPath] = {}; // Cache empty object for empty or whitespace-only files
      return {};
    }

    let json;
    try {
      json = JSON.parse(fileContent);
    } catch (err) {
      console.error('Error parsing the file content as JSON:', err);
      return {};
    }

    this.cache[resolvedPath] = json; // Cache parsed object
    return json;
  }
}
```

In this implementation, the ConfigReader class reads a configuration file synchronously and handles errors by logging error messages to console and returning an empty object. File reading is cached in memory for improved performance with repeated reads within the same session. The readConfigFileSync method is designed as synchronous to provide thread-safe operation in single-threaded environments, but could be adapted to support promises or async/await syntax for multi-threading.

This implementation assumes that JSON data is expected to be in the configuration file, and does not attempt to parse files with different formats. It also does not perform any specific optimizations for large files beyond reading them synchronously. Error handling assumes a single threaded environment; if this function were being used in a multi-threaded environment, additional measures would need to be taken to ensure thread safety.
