["Here's a simple JavaScript module that follows all your requirements. It uses promises to handle asynchronous operations and caching to minimize network requests. However, note that this is a simplistic version of what you might need in a larger application. For a production-ready solution, you would probably want more sophisticated error handling, logging, and configuration options:

```javascript
const cache = {};
let envFlags; // global flag object

function loadScript(src) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        if (cache[src]) {
            resolve();
            return;
        }

        const script = document.createElement('script');
        script.type = 'text/javascript';
        script.async = true;
        script.onload = () => {
            cache[src] = true;
            resolve();
        };
        script.onerror = (err) => reject(err);

        script.src = src;
        document.head.appendChild(script);
    });
}

function loadScriptsInOrder(scripts, flagName) {
    // Check for feature flag before loading scripts
    if (flagName && !envFlags[flagName]) return Promise.resolve();

    return scripts.reduce((promiseChain, scriptSrc) => {
        return promiseChain.then(() => loadScript(scriptSrc));
    }, Promise.resolve());
}

// Fetch environment flags from server or another source and set them globally
fetch('https://yourserver/api/flags').then((response) => response.json())
    .then((flags) => { envFlags = flags; });
```

In this module, `loadScript()` checks if a script has already been loaded, and only loads it if necessary. It returns a promise that resolves when the script is successfully loaded or immediately resolves if the script was previously cached. 

`loadScriptsInOrder()` takes an array of scripts to load and a feature flag name. If the flag name is provided and the corresponding flag is not set in `envFlags`, it doesn't do anything (returns an already resolved promise). Otherwise, it uses Array#reduce to chain promises that load each script in sequence.

Finally, at startup, we fetch environment flags from a server and store them globally for use by other modules. In a real-world application, you might instead fetch the flags as needed or have some sort of module that fetches them on startup and makes them available to all modules. 

Please remember this is a very basic implementation, actual usage would require additional error handling, logging, etc. Also consider bundling your scripts for production with tools like Webpack for optimizing network requests.
