["Sure, here is a basic PHP script which uses `Dotenv` library for setting environment variables from `.env` file, loads vendor autoloader and handles multi-stage configuration process with caching mechanism to minimize repeated configuration reads during runtime:

```php
<?php
// First we load DotEnv package
require_once 'vendor/autoload.php';
$dotenv = Dotenv\Dotenv::createImmutable(__DIR__);
$dotenv->load();

class Config {
    private static $configurations = [];

    public static function get($key, $default = null) {
        if (!array_key_exists($key, self::$configurations)) {
            // Load from .env file
            $value = getenv($key);

            // If not set in .env file then use default value
            if ($value === false) $value = $default;
        } else {
            $value = self::$configurations[$key];
        }

        return $value;
    }

    public static function loadConfiguration($environment = 'development') {
        $baseConfigFilePath = __DIR__ . '/config/' . $environment . '.php';

        if (!file_exists($baseConfigFilePath)) {
            throw new Exception('Invalid environment: ' . $environment);
        }

        $configurations = require($baseConfigFilePath);

        // Overwrite with local configuration if exists
        $localConfigFilePath = __DIR__ . '/config/' . $environment . '.local.php';

        if (file_exists($localConfigFilePath)) {
            $localConfigurations = require($localConfigFilePath);

            self::$configurations = array_merge($configurations, $localConfigurations);
        } else {
            self::$configurations = $configurations;
        }
    }
}
```

The above script does not handle missing environment variables or incorrectly set ones. It's a good practice to validate all required configurations before initializing the application. You can use a separate validation library, write your own logic, or throw exceptions. 

This solution assumes that you have base configuration files in `config` directory with name as environment (e.g., 'development', 'staging', 'production') and local overrides if they exist with postfix '.local'. Each file should return an associative array of configurations for the given environment.
"]

