['Sure, here is a C# code block that accomplishes this task using the `System.Security.Cryptography` namespace for MD5 hashing and the `log4net` library for logging. The function `ComputeMD5HashStreaming` reads input from a stream in chunks to avoid memory constraints. It's designed to be thread-safe by not maintaining any shared state between calls, so it can safely be used in a high-concurrency environment without lock contention.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using log4net;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class MD5HashProcessor
{
    private static readonly ILog logger = LogManager.GetLogger(typeof(MD5HashProcessor));

    public async Task<string> ComputeMD5HashStreaming(Stream input)
    {
        using (var md5 = MD5.Create())
        {
            byte[] buffer = new byte[4096]; // 4KB buffer
            int bytesRead;
            do
            {
                bytesRead = await input.ReadAsync(buffer, 0, buffer.Length);

                if (bytesRead > 0)
                {
                    md5.TransformBlock(buffer, 0, bytesRead, buffer, 0);
                }
            } while (bytesRead > 0);

            md5.TransformFinalBlock(new byte[0], 0, 0); // Signal end of data

            var hash = BitConverter.ToString(md5.Hash).Replace("-", "").ToLowerInvariant();

            logger.InfoFormat("Computed MD5 Hash: {0}", hash);

            return hash;
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses `await` to ensure the UI thread is not blocked while waiting for the IO operation to complete, improving responsiveness in a high-concurrency environment. It also logs the computed MD5 hash with the `log4net` logging library. 

