#define FLUSH_INTERVAL 60 // Flush log buffer every minute

FILE* errorLog = NULL;

void logMessage(char *msgFormat, ...) {
    va_list args;
    char buffer[MAX_MSG_LEN];

    time_t now = time(NULL);
    struct tm *timeinfo = localtime(&now);
    strftime(buffer, sizeof(buffer), "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S", timeinfo);

    static int count = 0; // Unique identifier for tracking purposes
    va_start(args, msgFormat);
    vsnprintf(buffer + strlen(buffer), MAX_MSG_LEN - strlen(buffer) - 1, msgFormat, args);
    va_end(args);

    if (errorLog == NULL && access("error.log", F_OK) == 0) { // If file exists
        errorLog = fopen("error.log", "a");
    } else if (errorLog == NULL) { // File does not exist
        errorLog = fopen("error.log", "w");
    }

    fprintf(errorLog, "[%s] [%d]: %s\n", buffer, count++, msgFormat);

    if (ftell(errorLog) >= FLUSH_INTERVAL * 1024 * 1024) { // If file size is larger than FLUSH_INTERVAL MB
        fflush(errorLog);
    }
}
```

This code provides a function `logMessage` that formats and writes messages to the error log. The function uses variable arguments for message formatting and includes a timestamp in each log entry. The unique identifier is incremented with every call of the function. 

The function also manages an open file handle for writing logs efficiently, and flushes the buffer to disk when it reaches a specified size limit to prevent overflowing the available memory or losing data in case of application crashes. Note that this code assumes Unix-like operating systems with access() and fopen() functions available. 

For real-world applications, you might want to add additional error handling, synchronization mechanisms (if your program is multi-threaded), support for custom log file paths, and more advanced features like log rotation and compression. You may also consider using a proven logging library such as glog or spdlog if these features are crucial for your application.
