["Here is a simple implementation using NodeJS Streams. This module uses Transform stream for easy processing of data in chunks. It correctly handles incomplete chunks and padding when required, as well as efficient memory management:

```javascript
const { Transform } = require('stream');

class DataAggregator extends Transform {
    constructor(options) {
        super(options);

        // User-specified options, 1024 bytes by default.
        this._sizeLimit = options && options.sizeLimit || 1024;
        this._buffer = Buffer.alloc(this._sizeLimit);
        this._bytesRead = 0;
    }

    _transform(chunk, encoding, callback) {
        if (this._bytesRead >= this._sizeLimit) {
            return callback();  // Skip over additional chunks when we've already read enough.
        }

        let chunkSize = Math.min(chunk.length, this._sizeLimit - this._bytesRead);

        chunk.copy(this._buffer, this._bytesRead, 0, chunkSize);
        this._bytesRead += chunkSize;

        callback(); // Always call the callback when done processing a chunk.
    }

    _flush(callback) {
        if (this._bytesRead < this._sizeLimit) {
            // Padding with zeros.
            this._buffer = Buffer.concat([this._buffer, Buffer.alloc(this._sizeLimit - this._bytesRead)]);
        }

        this.push(this._buffer);  // Output the aggregated buffer.
        callback();  // Signal that we're done processing all chunks.
    }
}
```
This module can be used like any other stream:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
let inputStream = fs.createReadStream('/path/to/input.file');
let dataAggregator = new DataAggregator({ sizeLimit: 512 });
let outputStream = fs.createWriteStream('/path/to/output.file');

inputStream.pipe(dataAggregator).pipe(outputStream);
```
This implementation is efficient and optimized for large datasets by processing data in chunks, minimizing memory usage and maximizing performance. It's also asynchronous to seamlessly integrate with external libraries while maintaining optimal resource utilization. 

Note: This code does not handle edge cases such as invalid byte offsets, it assumes that all input data is valid. If there are error scenarios that need to be handled specifically (e.g., file system errors), they should be handled by the caller of this module and not the module itself. 

To further optimize this code for different data types or different use cases, additional logic may be needed. This is a simple implementation and could serve as a starting point depending on specific requirements and constraints. The trade-offs between memory efficiency, processing speed, and ease of integration with existing systems have been considered in the design.
