["Here's a basic implementation of an async function that traverses the DOM efficiently using Promises and caching mechanisms to avoid unnecessary computations. Please note this is a high-level solution and does not account for every scenario or optimization possibility. 

```javascript
let cache = new Map(); // Create a new cache object as a map (key-value pair)

function traverseDOM(selectors, depth = 0) {
    if (!Array.isArray(selectors)) return Promise.reject('Invalid input: selectors should be an array');

    let elements = []; // initialize elements array to store found elements

    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        try {
            for (let i = 0; i < selectors.length; i++) {
                if (!cache.has(selectors[i])) { // check cache first
                    const selectorElements = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(selectors[i]));

                    if (depth > 0 && depth > selectors.length - 1) { // check depth limit
                        for (let j = 0; j < selectorElements.length; j++) {
                            elements.push(...traverseDOM([selectorElements[j].children], depth - 1)); // recurse to children if not at max depth
                        }
                    } else {
                        elements.push(...selectorElements); // add found elements to main array
                    }

                    cache.set(selectors[i], selectorElements); // store in cache for future use
                } else {
                    const cached = cache.get(selectors[i]); 
                    elements.push(...cached); // if found in cache, add to main array
                }
            }
        } catch (err) {
            reject('An error occurred: ' + err);
        }

        resolve(elements); // return Promise with the elements array
    });
}
```

This function takes an array of selectors and traverses the DOM, returning a Promise that resolves to an array of matching element nodes. It uses a cache map to store previously queried selectors for future use, reducing redundant lookups in the same DOM section. 

However, please note this code does not currently handle external resource dependencies, such as fetching CSS rules from a CDN or other dynamic style resources. Error handling is also very basic and only provides console logging of errors when they occur. These are all potential areas for improvements and would need to be handled at a much higher level depending on the exact requirements and constraints of your project.
