["Here's how you might implement such a robust asynchronous reduction function in JavaScript:

```javascript
const pRetry = require('p-retry');
const pSettle = require('p-settle');

async function asyncReduce(array, initialValue, callback) {
  let accumulator = initialValue;

  const promises = array.map((element) => {
    return pRetry(() => 
      callback(accumulator, element),
      { retries: 3 } // retry three times before failing
    );
  });

  const results = await pSettle(promises);

  for (let i = 0; i < results.length; i++) {
    if (results[i].isFulfilled) {
      accumulator = results[i].value; // update the accumulator value
    } else {
      console.error(`Error encountered: ${results[i].reason}`);
    }
  }

  return accumulator;
}
```

This function uses the `p-retry` library to retry failed operations three times before ultimately failing, and `p-settle` to collect all results in the form of an array. It logs any errors that occur during the process for debugging purposes. Note that this is a basic implementation and may need further refinement according to your specific requirements. 

