['Here's an example of how you could implement this using C#. This example assumes you are already familiar with the basics of CSV parsing in .NET, CSV Helper is a popular package for efficient CSV reading that also handles most edge cases automatically like missing values and quoted strings. 

Please note that implementing multi-threading, error handling, and network access would add significant complexity to this example code. It's best to break down the problem into smaller tasks, test each part individually, and then combine them. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using CsvHelper;
using System.Linq;

public class Record
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
}

public class CSVProcessor
{
    private List<Record> records = new List<Record>();

    // Assumes the file is small enough to fit into memory
    public void ProcessCSV(string csvFilePath, Func<Record, bool> filter)
    {
        using (var reader = new StreamReader(csvFilePath))
        using (var csv = new CsvReader(reader))
        {
            records = csv.GetRecords<Record>().Where(filter).ToList();
        }
    }

    // Assumes the file is too large to fit into memory, but read line by line
    public void ProcessCSVStreaming(string csvFilePath, Func<Record, bool> filter)
    {
        using (var reader = new StreamReader(csvFilePath))
        using (var csv = new CsvReader(reader))
        {
            while (csv.Read())
            {
                var record = csv.GetRecord<Record>();
                if (filter(record))
                    records.Add(record);
            }
        }
    } 
}
```

For the multi-threading, shared resources and network-based access, you may want to look into libraries or frameworks such as TPL (Task Parallel Library), Concurrent Collections etc. These can help manage large files efficiently and concurrently without crashing or producing incorrect results due to synchronization issues. 

