["Here is an example in C language which covers some of your requirements such as adding/updating connections with concurrent access protection, handling duplicate IP addresses, fetching connection details by IP address, etc. Note that this does not cover all the requirements mentioned in detail like integrating with an external configuration management service, error handling, memory optimization and ensuring scalability. You might want to consider using a high-level language such as Python or Java which have more comprehensive libraries for these tasks.

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct NICP {
    char ip[16]; // assuming IPv4 address is represented as a string of max 15 chars + 1 for null terminator
    int port;
    char status[20]; // assuming we can store connection status in a fixed size array
    struct NICP* next;
} NICP;

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
NICP* head = NULL;

void addConnection(char* ip, int port, char* status) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // to ensure thread-safe operations

    NICP* curr;

    // Check if the connection already exists. If so, update it instead of adding a new one.
    for(curr = head; curr != NULL; curr = curr->next) {
        if(!strcmp(curr->ip, ip)) {
            strncpy(curr->status, status, sizeof(curr->status)-1); // to ensure no buffer overflow
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // unlock the mutex before returning from function
            return;
        }
    }

    NICP* newConn = malloc(sizeof(NICP)); 
    strncpy(newConn->ip, ip, sizeof(newConn->ip)-1); // to ensure no buffer overflow
    newConn->port = port;
    strncpy(newConn->status, status, sizeof(newConn->status)-1); // to ensure no buffer overflow

    newConn->next = head; 
    head = newConn;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // unlock the mutex before returning from function
}

NICP* getConnectionByIP(char* ip) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // to ensure thread-safe operations

    NICP* curr;

    for(curr = head; curr != NULL; curr = curr->next) {
        if(!strcmp(curr->ip, ip)) {
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // unlock the mutex before returning from function
            return curr; 
        }
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // unlock the mutex before returning from function
    return NULL; // Return NULL if no matching connection was found
}
```
This implementation uses a singly-linked list to store and manage NICP structures. The `addConnection` function first checks for an existing connection with the same IP address, updating it if one is found, or else adding a new connection at the head of the list. The `getConnectionByIP` function simply iterates through the linked list until it finds a matching IP address, or returns NULL if no such connection exists.
Please note that this is just a simple example and doesn't cover all your requirements completely. It also lacks error handling which you would probably want to implement in a real-world system.
