['The problem you've described involves quite a bit of complexity and isn't easily solved with a single function. Here is a simplified version that reads from an input file, parses it line by line, counts the number of lines and writes this count to an output file. The rules can be customized using a string representing the set of aggregation rules. 

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

void process_log(char* input_filename, char* output_filename, char* rule_set, int verbose) {
    FILE *input = fopen(input_filename, "r");
    if (input == NULL) {
        printf("Could not open the input file\n");
        return;
    }

    FILE *output = fopen(output_filename, "w");
    if (output == NULL) {
        printf("Could not open the output file\n");
        return;
    }

    char* line = NULL;
    size_t len = 0;
    ssize_t read;

    int count = 0;
    while ((read = getline(&line, &len, input)) != -1) {
        if (strstr(line, rule_set) != NULL) {
            count++;
            if (verbose) {
                printf("Line matches the rules. Count: %d\n", count);
            }
        }
    }

    fprintf(output, "Number of lines that match the rule set: %d\n", count);

    free(line);
    fclose(input);
    fclose(output);
}
```

This is a simple example and a real-world application would likely be much more complex. The log entries might have to be parsed into structured data, the rules could require different types of operations (like addition, comparison etc.), and so on. 

