Java class:
```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import javax.xml.bind.DatatypeConverter;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.LogManager;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.Logger;

public class FileHasher {
    private static final Logger logger = LogManager.getLogger(FileHasher.class);

    public String calculateMD5Hash(String filePath, long offset) throws Exception {
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");

        try (InputStream is = new FileInputStream(filePath)) {
            byte[] buffer = new byte[8192];

            if (offset > 0) {
                long skipped = is.skip(offset);

                if (skipped < offset) {
                    throw new IOException("Only able to skip " + skipped + " bytes");
                }
            }

            int numRead;
            do {
                numRead = is.read(buffer);
                if (numRead <= 0) break;

                md.update(buffer, 0, numRead);
            } while (is.available() > 0);
        }

        return DatatypeConverter.printHexBinary(md.digest()).toUpperCase();
    }
}
```
Python function:
```python
import hashlib

def calculate_file_hash(filename, callback=None):
    BLOCKSIZE = 65536
    hasher = hashlib.md5()

    with open(filename, 'rb') as afile:
        buf = afile.read(BLOCKSIZE)

        while len(buf) > 0:
            hasher.update(buf)

            if callback is not None:
                callback(afile.tell())

            buf = afile.read(BLOCKSIZE)

    return hasher.hexdigest()
```

This code calculates the MD5 hash of a file in Python and Java, both support resuming from an offset and providing progress updates via a callback function. 

