["Here's a simplified implementation of what you asked for in C language. The code handles TCP connection stats management as described in your requirements. It uses an array-based approach with sorting based on `BYTES_RECEIVED` in descending order to optimize data retrieval.

```C
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    int bytesSent;
    int bytesReceived;
    time_t connEstablishmentTime;
    time_t lastActiveTimestamp;
} TCP_CONNECTION_STATS_T;

// Initialize the array and its size
#define INITIAL_ARRAY_SIZE 10
int currentArraySize = INITIAL_ARRAY_SIZE;
TCP_CONNECTION_STATS_T* tcpConnectionStatsArr = (TCP_CONNECTION_STATS_T*) malloc(sizeof(TCP_CONNECTION_STATS_T) * currentArraySize);
int numConnections = 0; // keep track of the number of connections

void manageTcpConnectionStats(TCP_CONNECTION_STATS_T newConnectionStat) {
    if (numConnections == currentArraySize) { 
        // Resize array when it's full
        currentArraySize *= 2;
        tcpConnectionStatsArr = realloc(tcpConnectionStatsArr, sizeof(TCP_CONNECTION_STATS_T) * currentArraySize);
    }

    // Add the new connection to the end of the array
    memcpy(&tcpConnectionStatsArr[numConnections], &newConnectionStat, sizeof(TCP_CONNECTION_STATS_T));
    numConnections++;

    // Sort the array by bytes received in descending order using bubble sort (inefficient) for simplicity. 
    // For better performance, you can use a more efficient algorithm such as quick sort or merge sort, or even a tree-based data structure like an AVL tree
    int i, j;
    TCP_CONNECTION_STATS_T temp;

    for (i = 0; i < numConnections - 1; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < numConnections - i - 1; j++) {
            if (tcpConnectionStatsArr[j].bytesReceived < tcpConnectionStatsArr[j + 1].bytesReceived) {
                temp = tcpConnectionStatsArr[j];
                tcpConnectionStatsArr[j] = tcpConnectionStatsArr[j + 1];
                tcpConnectionStatsArr[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

For the other points of your requirements, you need to implement a more robust system including concurrency control mechanisms (for handling incoming network events from multiple sources), handling data volumes and processing demands, error handling, logging and monitoring capabilities etc. This is quite an extensive task that requires much more code than just one function, but hopefully this gives you a starting point.
