Here's a very simplified example of how you might start to implement the first requirement (resolving a given hostname into its IP address) in C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <netdb.h>

void resolve_host(const char *hostname) {
    struct addrinfo hints, *res;

    memset(&hints, 0, sizeof(hints));
    hints.ai_family = AF_UNSPEC; // IPv4 and IPv6
    hints.ai_socktype = SOCK_STREAM;

    if (getaddrinfo(hostname, "80", &hints, &res) != 0) {
        perror("getaddrinfo");
        return;
    }

    char ip[INET6_ADDRSTRLEN];  // for IPv6 address

    if (res->ai_family == AF_INET) {   // IPv4
        struct sockaddr_in *ipv4 = (struct sockaddr_in *)res->ai_addr;
        inet_ntop(AF_INET, &(ipv4->sin_addr), ip, INET_ADDRSTRLEN);
    } else {  // IPv6
        struct sockaddr_in6 *ipv6 = (struct sockaddr_in6 *)res->ai_addr;
        inet_ntop(AF_INET6, &(ipv6->sin6_addr), ip, INET6_ADDRSTRLEN);
    }

    printf("IP address for host %s is: %s\n", hostname, ip);

    freeaddrinfo(res);  // release the linked list
}
```

This function uses getaddrinfo to resolve a hostname into an IP address. The hints structure specifies that both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses are acceptable. If getaddrinfo is successful, it converts the resolved address from network byte order (as stored in the sockaddr structures) into a human-readable format using inet_ntop.

Note: This example does not include any error handling beyond basic NULL pointer checks, and doesn't handle IPv6 addresses or other aspects of DNS that might be relevant to your requirements. It also doesn't implement the caching mechanism or configuration management system you requested. As mentioned earlier, it may not be appropriate or efficient for a production environment, but it can serve as a starting point. For more complex tasks like these, consider using higher-level languages or libraries.
