['Here is a simple implementation using C++ with POSIX threads for thread safety and locking mechanisms.

```c++
#include <pthread.h>
#include <map>
#include <string>

// Structure to hold network configuration details
struct NetworkConfig {
    std::string ip;
    std::string subnetMask;
    std::string gateway;
    int portRangeStart;
    int portRangeEnd;
};

typedef std::map<std::string, NetworkConfig> NetworkMap;
pthread_mutex_t mapLock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER; // Lock for thread safety
NetworkMap networkMap; // Global dynamic list of network configurations

// Add new configuration to the list, ensuring uniqueness by checking if ID already exists
void addConfiguration(std::string id, NetworkConfig config) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mapLock); // Lock before adding

    // Check if unique identifier is in use
    if (networkMap.find(id) != networkMap.end()) {
        std::cout << "ID already exists, choose a new one" << std::endl;
    } else {
        networkMap[id] = config; // Add configuration to map
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mapLock); // Unlock after adding
}

// Update existing configuration in the list by ID
void updateConfiguration(std::string id, NetworkConfig newConfig) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mapLock); // Lock before updating

    // Check if ID exists in map
    if (networkMap.find(id) == networkMap.end()) {
        std::cout << "ID does not exist" << std::endl;
    } else {
        networkMap[id] = newConfig; // Update configuration
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mapLock); // Unlock after updating
}
```
This code snippet implements a thread-safe dynamic list for managing network configurations. It uses a map (std::map) to store the configurations, where each key is a unique identifier and the value is the NetworkConfig struct that holds all other configuration details. The addConfiguration function checks if an ID already exists before adding it to the map to ensure uniqueness. Similarly, updateConfiguration checks if the ID exists in the map before updating its configuration. The locking mechanism (pthread_mutex) ensures thread safety by preventing race conditions during concurrent read/write operations on the map.
']

