```python
import java.security.MessageDigest
import java.math.BigInteger
import concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong

class HashUtils {
    private static final AtomicLong hashCount = new AtomicLong();

    public synchronized String sha256(String input) {
        try {
            MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
            byte[] digest = md.digest(input.getBytes());
            BigInteger number = new BigInteger(1, digest);
            StringBuilder hexString = new StringBuilder(number.toString(16));

            while (hexString.length() < 32) {
                hexString.insert(0, '0');
            }

            hashCount.incrementAndGet();

            return hexString.toString();
        } catch(Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }

    public long getHashCount() {
        return hashCount.get();
    }
}
```
This code is in Java and uses the built-in `java.security` package to compute SHA-256 hashes. It's thread safe by synchronizing on `this`. The counter `hashCount` stores how many times `sha256` method was called, which can be useful for tracking cumulative hash computations.
